This repository is not under a single licence. It has three layers, with three
different owners, and the layer decides what you may do.

Layer 1 -- the build scripts
    scripts/build.mjs, scripts/verify.mjs
    MIT. Copyright (c) 2026 Formative Intelligence Inc.
    Full text: LICENSES/MIT.txt

Layer 2 -- our curation
    Which 150 prompts are here, the nine use-case categories, the slugs, the
    ordering, every word of prose we wrote (README.md and its translations,
    CONTRIBUTING.md, TAKEDOWN.md, provenance/README.md, content/**), and the
    selection and arrangement expressed in data/prompts.json.
    CC BY 4.0. Copyright (c) 2026 Formative Intelligence Inc.
    Full text: LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt

Layer 3 -- the prompts themselves
    The 150 prompt bodies and their titles, wherever they appear: in
    data/prompts.json, in prompts/*.md, and quoted in any README.

    WE DID NOT WRITE THESE AND WE DO NOT LICENSE THEM TO YOU.

    Each was written by the person named on its row in ATTRIBUTION.md and first
    published by them in the post linked there. That file also records which
    collection we received each prompt through, and what that collection stated
    about its own copy -- CC0-1.0 for the 115 received via EvoLinkAI, CC BY 4.0
    for the 35 received via YouMind-OpenLab. We publish in reliance on those
    statements and on the fact that each prompt was published publicly by its
    author, with credit and a link back on every row.

    We have not independently established the chain of rights behind each
    prompt, and we make no representation to you about it. If you want to reuse
    a prompt for something that matters, ATTRIBUTION.md tells you whose work it
    is and where to find them.

    For the 35 rows received under CC BY 4.0, ATTRIBUTION.md carries the
    notices that licence asks a redistributor to retain: the creator as the
    licensor supplied it, the copyright notice, the reference to the licence,
    the warranty disclaimer, a link to the material at the commit we took it
    from, and a link to the licence text. We did not modify the prompt text.

A note on the badge
    GitHub reads the file you are reading now, does not recognise it, and will
    label this repository "Other" (SPDX: NOASSERTION). That is expected. A
    machine-readable map of which licence applies to which path is in
    REUSE.toml, which SPDX-aware scanners read instead.

    We could have made the badge say something tidy by putting one licence over
    the whole repository. That is what the collections upstream of us did, and
    it is the thing this file exists not to do: neither of them wrote the
    prompts either.

Removals and corrections
    TAKEDOWN.md. If you wrote one of these prompts and want it gone, we will
    remove it -- you do not need to explain why.
